            <ArticleTitle>Proctalgia fugax.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2238-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Proctalgia fugax is a fairly common but little-known cause of rectal pain. It is a benign condition that has no known etiology. Symptoms consist of episodic, sudden, sharp pain in the anorectal area, usually lasting several seconds to several minutes. Diagnosis is based on a history that fits the classic picture, coupled with a normal physical examination. Several treatments have been tried and found anecdotally to be effective, although reassurance is the most useful therapeutic option.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
